-Observations:
+Observations (revised after R1):
 
-1. At layer 0 the prefix position of an empty cell holds the same token in both pair members, so the shared readout gives identical predictions and W-AUC exactly 0.5. The teacher Z<=6 is identical across members by construction, also exactly 0.5.
-2. The whole-layer readout at P0 is perfect: at layer 0 the 20 peer positions hold the peers' raw clue tokens, and the candidate mask is a thresholded linear function of the peer clues. On the prefix this readout measures the input, not the model's computation.
-3. Controls with no readable signal (Brier 0.230–0.235, exact match 0) still give W-AUC between 0.43 and 0.58 whenever the representation differs between members. Bits within one pair are strongly correlated, so a pooled bit-level W-AUC has a much wider null spread than 0.5 plus binomial noise.
-4. Every pilot fit selected the grid edge (weight decay 0, learning rate 0.003) except some global fits, as in D96.
+1. At layer 0 the prefix position of an empty cell holds the same token in both pair members, and the teacher Z<=6 is identical across members by construction, so the shared readout gets identical inputs. The saved W-AUCs are nevertheless 0.50002–0.50004, not exactly 0.5: the two members are predicted in one float32 batch, and 1-ULP differences turn some exact ties into wins or losses.
+2. The whole-layer readout at P0 is perfect: at layer 0 the 20 peer positions hold the peers' raw clue tokens, and the candidate mask is a thresholded linear function of the peer clues. At P0 this readout measures the input; at higher prefix layers it is diagnostic only (the states are contextualized, but the input stays reachable through the residual stream).
+3. The controls are systematically shifted, not noisy around 0.5: P1 permuted has a per-pair mean of 0.432 (pair-level SE 0.0035, 219 of 250 pairs below 0.5), P2 labels 0.581 (SE 0.0034). Bit clustering within pairs does not explain this. Plausible causes: a fixed probe direction learned on one permutation (seed 11) shifts every pair the same way, and the label control selects its configuration and epoch on the true PROBE-VAL labels (`e3controls.fit_cells` permutes only the training labels). The E3 controls are therefore diagnostics, not a calibrated null.
+4. Selected configurations differ by representation: 23 of 53 fits chose (0.1, 0.001), including every Z<=6 fit (best epochs 2–8 of 80, early stopping, no budget cap reached); 21 chose (0, 0.003) (prefix shared P1–P8, prefix whole, B1–B4); 9 chose (0.1, 0.003).
+5. On PROBE-VAL, 6,313 of the 16,482 discordant bits in pairs with different level 1–6 codes (and 15,249 of 25,418 over all pairs) sit in cells whose generated Z<=6 cell state is identical in both members. With ties scored 0.5, the mean per-pair W-AUC of any own-position Z<=6 readout is bounded by 0.7004 over all pairs and 0.8093 over code-different pairs.
 
-## Draft decision D101
+## Draft decision D101 (revised after R1)
 
-1. Primary readout: the shared head on the cell's own position (D55), for every representation (generated Z<=6 and the prefix layers). Reason: observation 2 makes the whole-layer readout uninterpretable on the prefix, and the state-versus-model comparison needs one readout for both. Secondary: the whole-layer readout on Z<=6, which keeps the comparison with the manuscript's H1 primary probe (D66–D68). Descriptive only: the whole-layer readout on the prefix and the global BOS readout.
-2. Inference unit and null: the pair. The primary quantity is the mean over pairs of the per-pair W-AUC, compared with 0.5 by a pair-level sign-flip permutation test (swapping the two members maps a pair's W-AUC w to 1 − w) and a pair-level bootstrap interval. Secondary: per-pair difference between the representation's W-AUC and its own permuted-control W-AUC. Reason: observation 3.
-3. Prefix layer: one layer fixed by a rule before TEST: the layer with the highest mean per-pair W-AUC on the PROBE-VAL pairs (P2 in this pilot); TEST scores only that layer; the P0–P8 curve is descriptive.
-4. Z<=6 primary stratum: pairs whose generated level 1–6 codes differ; the code-identical pairs (W-AUC 0.5 by construction) are reported as a negative control and not pooled into the primary estimate.
-5. Remaining pilot items, restricted to where a weak readout could be a probe-fit artefact: a 4x-budget refit of the selected configuration and a converged full-batch L-BFGS L2 logistic reference for Z<=6 generated (shared and whole), B3 and P8; online MDL code length (Voita & Titov 2020) for Z<=6 generated, P0, P2 and B3 with the shared head (global head for B3).
-6. Not decided here: the activation-exchange causal test and its random-subspace control (D102).
+1. Primary readout: the shared head on the cell's own position (D55), for the generated Z<=6 and the prefix layers. Reason: the whole-layer readout measures the input at P0 (observation 2), and the state-versus-model comparison needs one readout family for both. The claim is limited to own-position linear readability with this probe family; the two inputs differ in space and dimension (Z<=6: 256-d sum of codebook contributions; prefix: 512-d residual stream), so equal readability is not equal capacity. Readability in the prefix shows that the one-step candidate mask is present in the puzzle encoding, not that the generator uses it (causal use: D102). Secondary: the whole-layer readout on Z<=6 (comparison with the manuscript's H1 probe, D66–D68). Descriptive only: whole-layer readouts on the prefix and the global BOS readout.
+2. Primary statistic and inference: the paired contrast Delta = W(P_k*) − W(Z<=6), with W the mean of per-pair W-AUCs and both terms computed on the same pairs with the same weighting, and a pair-level bootstrap interval that resamples whole pairs (blocks = solution symmetry orbits if a TEST orbit repeats). Two pre-registered parts, with thresholds fixed in the protocol: (a) Delta exceeds a minimum meaningful difference; (b) W(Z<=6) lies within an equivalence band around the value of a representation without within-pair information (TOST at the pair level). The sample size is planned for (b). No sign-flip test (swapping members leaves a pair's W-AUC unchanged). The E3 permuted and label controls are reported as diagnostics (observation 3); if a control is used as an empirical null, the whole fit-and-select procedure is repeated over many independent permutations, and a label control permutes the tuning labels as well.
+3. Prefix layer: fixed before TEST by the rule: highest mean per-pair W-AUC on the PROBE-VAL pairs; ties broken by lower PROBE-VAL Brier, then by the lower layer index (in the pilot: P2). The layer, preprocessing and fitted probes are frozen before TEST; only that layer is scored on TEST; the P0–P8 curve is descriptive. Interval rule at the bound: when fewer than 20 pairs have a per-pair W-AUC below 1 (or, for Z<=6, different from 0.5), a conservative exact bound replaces the bootstrap (as plan v3.7 §11.2).
+4. Population: the primary analysis uses all pairs for both representations. Secondary, for both: the stratum of pairs whose generated level 1–6 codes differ; the code-identical stratum is a negative control for Z<=6. Also reported: the share of discordant bits whose local Z<=6 cell state is identical in both members and the W-AUC restricted to the other bits (observation 5).
+5. Scoring rules: predictions are compared in float64, and verified bit-identical inputs count as ties; a non-finite prediction on an evaluable bit stops the run; pairs without a discordant bit are excluded and counted, an empty stratum is reported as not estimable (null); outputs give both the per-pair means and the bit-pooled values.
+6. Remaining pilot items, restricted to where a weak readout could be a fit artefact: a converged full-batch L-BFGS L2 logistic reference on inputs standardized with PROBE-TRAIN statistics, over a grid of regularization strengths, for Z<=6 (shared and whole), P2 and P8; online MDL code length (Voita & Titov 2020) for Z<=6, P0 and P2 with the shared head. The 4x AdamW refit is dropped (the Z<=6 fits stop early at epochs 2–8, so it would not change them), and B3 is dropped (descriptive readout).
+7. Deferred to the protocol design: a readout for the level-7 query positions (they read both Z<=6 and the prefix; they do not map to cells), and a breakdown by inference-wave depth.
+8. Not decided here: the activation-exchange causal test and its random-subspace control (D102).
 
